  "Number": 18,
  "Question": "If \\(x^2-5x+6\\lt0\\) and \\(P=x^2+5x+6,\\) then",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(P\\) can take any real value",
    "B": "\\(20\\lt P\\lt30\\)",
    "C": "\\(0\\lt P\\lt20\\)",
    "D": "\\(P\\lt0\\)",
    "E": "\\(P\\gt30\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "First factor the given quadratic to find the allowed values of \\(x\\):\n\\[\n(x-2)(x-3)<0.\n\\]\nThe product is negative exactly when the two factors have opposite signs, which occurs for \\(2<x<3\\). At either endpoint the product is zero, so neither endpoint is allowed.\n\nNow factor the expression we want to bound:\n\\[\nP=(x+2)(x+3).\n\\]\nFrom \\(2<x<3\\), we have \\(4<x+2<5\\) and \\(5<x+3<6\\). All these quantities are positive, so multiplying their bounds gives\n\\[\n20=4\\cdot5<(x+2)(x+3)<5\\cdot6=30.\n\\]\nThus \\(20<P<30\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
